From 5e29dad968aeb3e37b5dad58514753213fa8b01a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Mohamed R. Khalil" <m.radwan.khalil@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 19:02:41 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] fix(v2): give projected v1 tools a working ask() through the
 v2 permission system

Fix 23 stopped the 'ctx?.ask is not a function' crash but skipped the skill
permission prompt entirely on v2. The plugin host narrows ctx.permission to
hook/list/get/reply - there is no public request-creation API for a plugin
tool - and the projected tool context carries no ask.

The platform does expose Permission.ask over its external HTTP surface
(POST /api/session/{id}/permission): it evaluates session/agent rules,
parks a real pending request that clients prompt on (permission.asked),
and the outcome returns as a permission.replied event on ctx.event. The
bridge implements the v1 ask() contract on top of that:

- allow -> resolves; deny -> SkillPermissionDeniedError (no prompt)
- ask -> the user is prompted; once/always resolves, reject throws,
  and an aborted tool call rejects its own parked request best-effort
- no reachable server origin, or a create failure before anything was
  shown -> the fix-23 logged skip (documented fail-open)

Origin resolution extends serve-http.ts's lsof approach with a netstat
fallback so embedded Windows servers (OpenChamber) resolve too. tools.ts
is unchanged: fix 23's typeof-ask guard picks the bridge-provided ask up
automatically, and v1 hosts are untouched.
